
The task is to co-write pluk. Pluk is a Rust implementation of pick. Pick is a command-line utility.
It can be found in the subdirectory pick, its tests are in pick/test , documentation in pick/doc/ .

I am not a Rust programmer, but aim to become one. The goal of the rewrite is to have a clear development path
and commit history that make sense to me and will aid me in understanding the code.  For context, I am a
reasonably experienced C and Perl programmer and am comfortable discussing coding designs.  I will ask clarification
when needed.  My questions and comments on pluk development should be seen as very separate from my questions
about Rust coding. I have firm opinions on the former and am an equal partner in discussions, however, I am just a
beginning Rust coder. One of my aims is to use Claude and this project as accelerators towards learning Rust. My
mastering of Rust will quite likely not keep up with the pace of pluk development that I envision. This is fine.

The directory and file structure of this project is up for discussion and re-arrangement. I need a subdirectory
that allows me to write quick small exercises and test code. I need to be able to quickly add, say foo.rs
and compile it to an executable file. I propose utilising the existing subdirectory 'shed' for this.

Git commits will be done by me unless I specify otherwise.

The plan is executed in steps (with step files as described later) in guidelines.txt) with the aid of design files that
pin down the current state of certain decisions, e.g.  DATASTRUCTURE-cell.design.txt.  The seed for such a file may be
contained in a file with '.notes.txt' suffix, in this case DATASTRUCTURE-cell.notes.txt . The notes file contains some
thoughts of mine that should kick-start a discussion that leads to decisions documented in the design file.

It is crucial to follow the step plan. Feel free to suggest improvements to the plan.

The workflow will generally be to pin down a step file first and then implement what's in the step file.

The step files currently present probably amount to less than 8% of development time that is required.
The rest of the step files will be created later.

When commenting on steps, it is fine to take into account the full set of pick functionality that will
eventually need to be implemented.

Now check in with me to clear up anything that requires so.

Then proceed with guidelines.txt , then check back in again.

